## Simple (IF-THEN) rules: Remove action (retraction)

### Context
Removal rules existed only for Complex (SPARQL) rules. A Simple (IF-THEN) rule could only
assert triples. A THEN part can now remove triples: a rule with `action: "retract"` matches
the triples its IF part binds and hides them through the same removal overlay as a Complex
removal rule. Assertions are unchanged (`action` defaults to `"assert"`, so rules saved
before this change behave as before).

Decisions taken with the user:
- A retraction's THEN part accepts property triples and literal attribute values only. Class
  membership (`rdf:type`), built-ins and negation are refused.
- Data-quality checks skip retractions (a removal is not a violation check).
- A retraction matches the graph as it was before the run's inferences (same as the
  Complex removal rules in the same run).

### Changes
1. `src/back/core/w3c/shacl/constants.py`: `IF_THEN_ACTION_ASSERT`, `IF_THEN_ACTION_RETRACT`,
   `IF_THEN_ACTIONS`.
2. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/ShaclRule.py`: `RETRACT_RULE` rule type, `action`
   field and `is_retraction` property on `ShaclRule`.
3. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/IfThenToShaclTranspiler.py`: a retraction renders
   as a SPARQL `DELETE { … } WHERE { … }` (`construct_sparql`) and as a SHACL shape with
   `ex:action "retract"` and no `sh:rule` (SHACL-AF has no delete). `_build_construct` takes
   the verb.
4. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/ShaclFlatSqlBackend.py`: for a retraction the
   consequent SELECT keeps the triples that exist (`EXISTS`) instead of the missing ones
   (`NOT EXISTS`). A dangling object (not bound by the IF part) emits no SQL. The guard
   flip was caught by running the generated SQL on sample rows: without it a retraction
   matched nothing.
5. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/ShaclRuleSqlTranslator.py`: passes `retract` to the
   flat backend for a retraction rule.
6. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/ShaclRuleEngine.py`: `execute_rules` routes a
   retraction to `_retract_rule`, which collects the SELECT rows into `removed_triples`
   (never inferred, never materialised). The SELECT runs in `_select_rule_rows`, shared
   with `_infer_rule`. The removal is applied by `ReasoningService.materialize_inferred`
   (origin `reasoning`), unchanged.
7. `src/back/objects/ontology/Rules.py`: `validate_if_then_rule` checks the action and the
   retraction shape (`_retraction_errors`: property/attribute atoms only, no built-ins or
   negation, variables bound by the IF part). The tautology gate is skipped for retractions.
   The atom parser is shared (`_iter_atoms`).
8. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/rule_serializer.py`: the combined SHACL export omits
   retractions and says how many were omitted; `preview_rule_shacl` takes `action`.
9. `src/api/routers/internal/ontology.py`: `/ontology/shacl-rules/preview` forwards `action`;
   the import dedupe signature includes the action (an assertion and a retraction with the
   same text are different rules).
10. `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/quality/TwinDataQualityRun.py`: data-quality runs skip
    retractions.
11. `src/back/objects/digitaltwin/node/NodeBusinessRuleService.py`: a per-entity run of a
    retraction is refused with a clear message (only the reasoning pipeline applies removals).
12. `src/back/core/reasoning/shacl_rules/__init__.py`: exports `RETRACT_RULE`.
13. `src/front/templates/partials/ontology/_ontology_business_rules.html`: **Action** select
    (Assert / Remove) in the THEN pane and a retraction hint.
14. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-if-then.js`: the action is read on save and sent
    to the preview; restored when editing; the preview refreshes on change; the Simple list
    shows a **Remove** badge for retractions (reuses `br-kind-badge-removal`).
15. `docs/user-guide.md`: step 5 "Action (THEN)" in the IF-THEN section (steps renumbered).
16. `docs/api.md`: the `action` field of the save body and the preview route.

Not covered (by design): Neo4j. The Cypher IF-THEN translator is still a scaffold that returns
no SQL, so IF-THEN rules (assertions or retractions) do not run on Neo4j yet. Removals made
by a retraction return on the next reasoning run, the same as Complex removals (a Build does
not reapply them).

